{
  "version": 221,
  "released": "2026-08-26",
  "changelog": "v221: who is who, and which event is which — identity resolution that never destroys (wave C1 of the audited final timeline build plan, §6.3 and §10). WHY: v220 froze the claim contracts and left two questions open on purpose. A claim keeps its RAW mention forever — but nothing yet said which person \"AJ\" is, and nothing said whether \"we started dating in 2005\" and \"we married in 2007\" are one fact about a relationship or two facts about two events. Guessing either one destroys information no later correction can recover, so this release answers both with rules that fail loud instead of folding quietly. ONE NEW PURE MODULE, no I/O, no model, no vault: `system/identity_resolution.py`. The entity roster is taken as an ARGUMENT (a snapshot dict or its entity list) because `entity_roster` imports `lifehug_core`, `ai_provider` and `recommend_focuses`, and a resolver that drags vault paths and a provider along cannot be vendored into the worker or the sandboxed prompt seam; the alias DATA still has exactly one owner. THE RESOLUTION RECORD is the contract the deterministic rules and the Wave C model rung both write through, so a link's provenance reads the same however it was reached: mention, mention_key, candidates (each with its ref, display name and score-basis), resolution (same|different|uncertain), resolved_ref, a NAMED reason, evidence_ref, reversible, created_at. The refusals are the point — `same` must name a ref that was actually in the running (`resolved_ref_not_a_candidate`); `uncertain` may not smuggle one (`resolved_ref_forbidden`); ambiguity IS the candidate set (`ambiguous_needs_candidates`); and `reversible` is a property of the contract, not a choice a writer may decline (`resolution_not_reversible`). THE DETERMINISTIC LADDER is exact-match plus a uniqueness gate and nothing else: `exact_ref` (the mention is already a stable ref, so resolution is idempotent), then a single roster key match reported as `roster_alias` or `unique_name`, then `ambiguous_candidates`/`no_candidate`. There is NO containment folding and NO fuzzy matching — the prior audit rejected both, and a rule that can silently merge two people must not exist here. Alias matching is uniqueness-gated rather than a strict rung above name matching for the same reason: a roster where one person is NAMED \"Mom\" and another carries \"Mom\" as an alias yields two candidates and one honest `uncertain`, where an alias-first ladder would have picked silently. EPISODES. `derive_episode_ref` mints through `temporal_projection.derive_node_id` — the one identity function — so an episode ref IS the node id wave D publishes and a claim's `event_ref`, a Mirror row and a work item all point at the same thing. A relationship transition REQUIRES a counterpart and attaches to the order-normalized edge between two people, so first_met, dating_started, engaged, married, separated and reconciled are six identities on ONE `relationship_edge_ref` (event-kind-free, date-free) rather than one collapsed relationship. A repeatable kind (job, school, move, residence, military) REFUSES to mint without a discriminator, because a missing discriminator is exactly how a second stint at the same employer silently becomes an edit of the first; `episode_discriminator` draws it from the episode's OWN start claim. REVERSIBILITY IS MECHANICAL. `apply_resolution` returns a NEW claim dict and asserts the claim id did not move — the raw-mention identity of v220 is a property nothing checked until now, and a property nothing checks is a property waiting to break at the next pin bump. `unresolve` reverses a link without destroying it: the ref that won stays in the candidate set and the undone decision is kept in `reverses`, so undoing an undo is possible. An ambiguous mention mints an `identity_uncertain` TemporalWorkItem through v220's `derive_work_item_id`, keyed on an `unresolved:<mention key>` handle — a handle, never an entity ref — so every sighting of the same ambiguous name is ONE Mirror row. A name nobody in the roster resembles mints nothing: that is a person we have not met yet, not a disagreement, and the claim keeps its record either way. Deliberately NOT here: the model-assisted high-confidence nickname link (a wave C listener seam), and surfacing — wave D/E owns that. 63 tests write §10's identity and event scenarios verbatim.",
